 ],
 "literal": "She blessed (me) as I sneezed; (then) having retracted (it), she wept saying, \"Thinking of who did you sneeze?\"",
 "translation": "When I sneezed, she blessed me. But then she took it back and wept, asking, \"Who were you thinking of when you sneezed?\"",
 "modern": "நான் தும்மியபோது அவள் என்னை வாழ்த்தினாள். ஆனால் உடனே அந்த வாழ்த்தைத் திரும்பப் பெற்றுக்கொண்டு, \"யாரை நினைத்துக்கொண்டு நீங்கள் தும்மினீர்கள்?\" என்று கேட்டு அழுதாள்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet captures a playful lovers' quarrel based on a traditional folk belief. In Tamil culture, a sneeze is often thought to be caused by thinking of a loved one, or by a loved one thinking of you. When the speaker sneezes, his lover instinctively blesses him with good health. However, she quickly becomes jealous, retracts her blessing (indicated by the word 'aḻittu', meaning to destroy or undo), and weeps, asking which other woman he was thinking of to cause the sneeze.",
